Comando para instlar Keepalived
sudo apt install keepalived -y

Comando para verificar si la ip virtual que usaremos esta libre
ip neigh show 192.168.100.10

Comandos para configurar keepalicve en Server1
sudo nano /etc/keepalived/keepalived.conf

Dentro de nano
global_defs {
    router_id SERVER1
    script_user root
    enable_script_security
}

vrrp_script chk_apache {
    script "/usr/bin/systemctl is-active --quiet apache2"
    interval 2
    timeout 2
    fall 2
    rise 2
    weight -30
}

vrrp_instance VI_1 {
    state BACKUP
    interface ens33
    virtual_router_id 51
    priority 110
    advert_int 1

    authentication {
        auth_type PASS
        auth_pass HApass01
    }

    virtual_ipaddress {
        192.168.100.10/24 dev ens33
    }

    track_script {
        chk_apache
    }
}
En el server 2:
global_defs {
    router_id SERVER2
    script_user root
    enable_script_security
}

vrrp_script chk_apache {
    script "/usr/bin/systemctl is-active --quiet apache2"
    interval 2
    timeout 2
    fall 2
    rise 2
    weight -30
}

vrrp_instance VI_1 {
    state BACKUP
    interface ens37
    virtual_router_id 51
    priority 100
    advert_int 1

    authentication {
        auth_type PASS
        auth_pass HApass01
    }

    virtual_ipaddress {
        192.168.100.10/24 dev ens37
    }

    track_script {
        chk_apache
    }
}
Comando para iniciar el servicio keepalive:
sudo systemctl enable keepalived
sudo systemctl start keepalived

Comando para comprobar su estado:
sudo systemctl status keepalived

Para comprobar quien tiene la ip virtual usamos
ip addr show ens33

Si accedemos con la ip 
http://192.168.100.10
Debe de aparecer el HTML del server 1

Comando para apagar server1
sudo poweroff

Si volvemos a usar 
http://192.168.100.10
Aparece el server 2

